@page "/"
@using MeDotNet.Models
@inject PostService PostService
@inject PersistentComponentState ApplicationState
@implements IDisposable

<PageTitle>JasonObject — Jason St. John</PageTitle>

@* One heading only. The hook is the h1; the line under it is a sentence, not a
   sub-heading, and the byline is a caption. Stacking three descending type sizes
   read as three headers in a row.

   The line breaks are deliberate, not wrapping: the hook lands harder split across
   two lines, and the payoff separates the childhood fact from the thesis it sets up. *@
<h1 class="mud-typography mud-typography-h3" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem">
    My first computer<br />didn't play games.
</h1>

<MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-3" Style="font-size: 1.15rem; line-height: 1.6">
    So I learned BASIC and taught it to.<br />We've been teaching each other ever since.
</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.caption" Class="mud-text-secondary d-block mb-8">
    Jason St. John · lead full-stack developer · Upstate New York
</MudText>

<MudPaper Class="pa-4 mb-6 float-left" Elevation="1" Style="max-width: 320px">
    <MudImage Src="/img/jason-st-john.jpg" Alt="Jason St. John" Fluid="true" Width="800" Height="1099" Class="rounded" />
</MudPaper>

@* The record sits beside the photo, where the bio used to. It is the point of the
   site, so it gets the prominent slot; the bio moved below and full width. *@
<MudText Typo="Typo.overline" Class="mud-text-secondary d-block mb-4">Latest from the record</MudText>

        @if (_recent is null)
        {
            <MudProgressCircular Indeterminate="true" />
        }
        else if (_recent.Count == 0)
        {
            <MudText>The record starts soon.</MudText>
        }
        else
        {
            @foreach (var entry in _recent)
            {
                <RecordEntryRow Entry="entry" />
            }
            <MudButton Href="/notes" Variant="Variant.Text" EndIcon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ArrowForward">
                Read the whole record
            </MudButton>
        }

@* The site is called JasonObject. Rendering him as an actual object is the joke the
   name has been setting up, and it doubles as a scannable TLDR of the prose beside it.
   It is valid JSON on purpose: a developer will check, and a broken one would cost more
   than it earns. Every value is true. *@
@* Grid, not float. A float let the bio ride up beside the record instead of
   staying put; a grid pins both columns to the same row and clears everything
   above it once. Long values are broken across lines so the block never needs a
   horizontal scrollbar. *@
<div class="bio-block">
    <div class="json-card">
        <pre aria-label="Summary of Jason St. John as a JSON object"><span class="j-p">{</span>
  <span class="j-k">"name"</span><span class="j-p">:</span> <span class="j-s">"Jason R. St. John"</span><span class="j-p">,</span>
  <span class="j-k">"role"</span><span class="j-p">:</span> <span class="j-s">"lead full-stack developer"</span><span class="j-p">,</span>
  <span class="j-k">"years"</span><span class="j-p">:</span> <span class="j-n">12</span><span class="j-p">,</span>
  <span class="j-k">"builds"</span><span class="j-p">:</span>
    <span class="j-s">"the internal tools others work in"</span><span class="j-p">,</span>
  <span class="j-k">"stack"</span><span class="j-p">:</span> <span class="j-p">[</span>
    <span class="j-s">".NET"</span><span class="j-p">,</span> <span class="j-s">"Vue"</span><span class="j-p">,</span> <span class="j-s">"Nuxt"</span><span class="j-p">,</span>
    <span class="j-s">"TypeScript"</span><span class="j-p">,</span> <span class="j-s">"AWS"</span>
  <span class="j-p">],</span>
  <span class="j-k">"method"</span><span class="j-p">:</span>
    <span class="j-s">"spec, plan, implement, review"</span><span class="j-p">,</span>
  <span class="j-k">"learning"</span><span class="j-p">:</span> <span class="j-p">[</span><span class="j-s">"Rust"</span><span class="j-p">,</span> <span class="j-s">"Svelte"</span><span class="j-p">],</span>
  <span class="j-k">"taughtBy"</span><span class="j-p">:</span> <span class="j-s">"an agent, currently"</span><span class="j-p">,</span>
  <span class="j-k">"dogs"</span><span class="j-p">:</span> <span class="j-n">2</span>
<span class="j-p">}</span></pre>
    </div>

    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Eight years architecting production .NET and Vue/Nuxt platforms, a few years teaching
        school before that. Directing AI agents is an instruction problem: decomposition,
        scaffolding, feedback, and telling the difference between a model that misunderstood
        and one that wasn't given enough. Lately the teaching runs both ways; an agent is
        walking me through Rust.
    </MudText>
</div>

@code {
    private List<Post>? _recent;
    private PersistentLoader? _loader;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        _loader = new PersistentLoader(ApplicationState);
        _recent = await _loader.LoadAsync("home-recent",
            async () => (await PostService.GetPublishedAsync()).Take(3).ToList());
    }

    public void Dispose() => _loader?.Dispose();
}
